---
/**
 * Training batch 02 — a long-form editorial case study.
 *
 * Generated for review in the tuner's Training phase (dev only; never
 * built). Each section is a recipe, sometimes with its `choices`
 * overridden, or a bespoke section of layout primitives; `data-recipe`
 * and `data-variant` name it for comments. See the mast-train skill.
 */
import BaseLayout from "@/layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import Footer from "@/components/Footer.astro";
import Section from "@/components/Section.astro";
import Row from "@/components/Row.astro";
import Col from "@/components/Col.astro";
import Divider from "@/components/Divider.astro";
import Spacer from "@/components/Spacer.astro";
import Card from "@/components/Card.astro";
import CardBody from "@/components/CardBody.astro";
import Img from "@/components/Img.astro";
import Accordion from "@/components/Accordion.astro";
import ContentWrap from "@/components/ContentWrap.astro";
import Statement from "@/components/recipes/Statement.astro";
import Cta from "@/components/recipes/Cta.astro";
import { postSrcset } from "@/lib/blog.ts";

const stats = [
  { value: "41%", label: "less bread thrown away, store for store" },
  { value: "$310k", label: "saved on flour and labor in the first year" },
  { value: "0", label: "stores that ran out before 4pm" },
];
---

<BaseLayout title="Batch 02 · Editorial" description="Training page.">
  <Section tag="header" data-recipe="bespoke" data-variant="article-header">
    <Row>
      <Col size={10}>
        <div class="eyebrow cc-rule">Case study · Operations</div>
        <h1>The year Millbrook stopped baking for the bin</h1>
      </Col>
    </Row>
    <Spacer size="md" />
    <Row justify="between" align="end">
      <Col size={6}>
        <p class="paragraph-xl u-mb-0">
          A fourteen-store bakery threw out a fifth of everything it baked. The
          fix wasn't software. It was asking the people at the ovens.
        </p>
      </Col>
      <Col size="shrink">
        <p class="paragraph-sm u-mb-0">
          By Delia Fournier · 14 min read · March 2026
        </p>
      </Col>
    </Row>
  </Section>

  <Section class="u-pt-0" data-recipe="bespoke" data-variant="lead-image">
    <Card>
      <Img
        ratio="16x9"
        src="/images/post7.webp"
        alt=""
        loading="eager"
        sizes="(max-width: 47.9375rem) 100vw, 90vw"
        srcset={postSrcset("post7")}
      />
    </Card>
    <Spacer size="sm" />
    <Row justify="end">
      <Col size={4}>
        <p class="paragraph-sm u-mb-0">
          The 4am shift at Millbrook's central bakery in Dayton, where every
          store's bread starts its day.
        </p>
      </Col>
    </Row>
  </Section>

  <Section data-recipe="bespoke" data-variant="article-body">
    <Row justify="center">
      <Col size={7}>
        <div class="rich-text cc-lg">
          <p>
            Every evening at seven, the closing staff at Millbrook's Oakwood
            store carried six crates of unsold bread to the dumpster behind the
            building. Sourdough, rye, the seeded loaf customers asked for by
            name. On a bad night it was nine crates.
          </p>
          <p>
            "We all knew," says Tasha Greer, who has run the Oakwood counter for
            eleven years. "You could watch it. The ordering sheet said forty
            seeded on a Tuesday, and on Tuesdays nobody buys seeded."
          </p>
          <h2>A forecast nobody trusted</h2>
          <p>
            The ordering sheet came from a forecasting tool the company had
            bought in 2019. It looked at last year's sales and the weather, and
            it was usually close on Saturdays. On weekdays it was wrong in a way
            that only the counter staff could see: it didn't know about the
            school pickup, the hospital shift change, or the farmers' market two
            streets over.
          </p>
          <p>
            Store managers had learned to override it. Head bakers had learned
            to ignore the overrides. By 2024 the bakery was making three
            different guesses every night, and throwing out 19% of what it
            baked.
          </p>
        </div>
      </Col>
    </Row>
  </Section>

  <Section
    class="u-full-height-center u-text-white"
    data-recipe="bespoke"
    data-variant="bg-image-quote"
  >
    <Img
      ratio="bg"
      src="/images/post11.webp"
      alt=""
      sizes="100vw"
      srcset={postSrcset("post11")}
    />
    <Row justify="center">
      <Col size={9}>
        <div class="rich-text h3 u-text-center">
          <p>
            "Nobody asked the counter what was left at seven. The counter always
            knew."
          </p>
        </div>
        <p class="paragraph-sm u-text-center u-mb-0">
          Tasha Greer, counter lead, Oakwood
        </p>
      </Col>
    </Row>
  </Section>

  <Section data-recipe="bespoke" data-variant="article-body-aside">
    <Row justify="between" align="start">
      <Col size={3} class="u-position-sticky" style="top: 2rem;">
        <Card>
          <CardBody>
            <div class="eyebrow">What changed</div>
            <ul class="paragraph-sm u-mb-0">
              <li>A ten-second count at close, on paper</li>
              <li>Counts phoned in by 7:30pm</li>
              <li>Head baker sets the order, nobody else</li>
              <li>The forecast became a suggestion</li>
            </ul>
          </CardBody>
        </Card>
      </Col>
      <Col size={7} class="col-lg-offset-1 col-md-offset-0">
        <div class="rich-text cc-lg">
          <h2>Ten seconds at closing time</h2>
          <p>
            The change, when it came, was almost embarrassingly small. In
            February 2025, operations lead Ravi Menon asked every store to count
            what was left on the shelves at close and call the number in to the
            central bakery. Not a scan, not an app: a tally on the back of the
            ordering sheet, read out over the phone.
          </p>
          <p>
            Head baker Luis Ortega took the calls himself for the first month.
            "I had a whiteboard with fourteen columns," he says. "By week three
            I could see Oakwood's Tuesdays. By week six I could see the rain."
          </p>
          <blockquote>
            The forecast never knew about the hospital shift change. Luis
            learned it in a fortnight.
          </blockquote>
          <p>
            Within two months, Ortega was setting each store's order from the
            previous week's leftovers, with the forecast as a starting point
            rather than an instruction. The counts took ten seconds. The calls
            took four minutes. Waste fell every month that spring.
          </p>
        </div>
      </Col>
    </Row>
  </Section>

  <Section class="u-mode-dark" data-recipe="bespoke" data-variant="stats-row">
    <Row>
      <Col size={6}>
        <div class="eyebrow cc-rule">Twelve months later</div>
      </Col>
    </Row>
    <Spacer size="md" />
    <Row justify="between" gap="main">
      {
        stats.map((stat) => (
          <Col size={3}>
            <div class="h1 u-mb-xs">{stat.value}</div>
            <p class="paragraph-sm u-mb-0">{stat.label}</p>
          </Col>
        ))
      }
    </Row>
  </Section>

  <Section data-recipe="bespoke" data-variant="paired-images">
    <Row align="end">
      <Col size={5}>
        <Card>
          <Img
            ratio="3x4"
            src="/images/post1.webp"
            alt=""
            sizes="(max-width: 47.9375rem) 100vw, 40vw"
            srcset={postSrcset("post1")}
          />
        </Card>
        <p class="paragraph-sm u-mt-sm u-mb-0">
          Before: Oakwood's back step at 7pm, February 2025.
        </p>
      </Col>
      <Col size={7}>
        <Card>
          <Img
            ratio="4x3"
            src="/images/post12.webp"
            alt=""
            sizes="(max-width: 47.9375rem) 100vw, 55vw"
            srcset={postSrcset("post12")}
          />
        </Card>
        <p class="paragraph-sm u-mt-sm u-mb-0">
          After: the same shelves a year on, with one crate left for the food
          bank pickup.
        </p>
      </Col>
    </Row>
  </Section>

  <Statement
    choices={{ composition: "editorial", alignment: "left" }}
    text="The best data in the company was walking to the dumpster every night."
    attribution="— Ravi Menon, operations lead"
  />

  <Section theme="alt" data-recipe="bespoke" data-variant="notes-accordion">
    <Row justify="center">
      <Col size={7}>
        <h2 class="h4">Notes on method</h2>
        <ContentWrap role="list">
          <Accordion
            title="How waste was measured"
            name="method"
            role="listitem"
            class="u-mb-sm"
          >
            <div class="rich-text">
              <p>
                Unsold units at close, counted by hand, divided by units
                delivered that morning. Donated bread counts as waste in these
                figures, so the real loss to landfill fell further.
              </p>
            </div>
          </Accordion>
          <Accordion
            title="Which stores were compared"
            name="method"
            role="listitem"
            class="u-mb-sm"
          >
            <div class="rich-text">
              <p>
                All fourteen stores, March 2024–February 2025 against March
                2025–February 2026. Two stores that changed opening hours are
                excluded from the store-for-store figure.
              </p>
            </div>
          </Accordion>
          <Accordion
            title="Where the savings came from"
            name="method"
            role="listitem"
          >
            <div class="rich-text">
              <p>
                Roughly two-thirds from flour, butter, and seeds not bought;
                one-third from overnight bake hours no longer needed. The bakery
                did not cut any jobs.
              </p>
            </div>
          </Accordion>
        </ContentWrap>
        <Divider size="main" />
        <p class="paragraph-sm u-mb-0">
          Reporting by Delia Fournier. Photographs by Sam Achterberg. Figures
          supplied by Millbrook Bakery and checked against store records.
        </p>
      </Col>
    </Row>
  </Section>

  <Cta
    choices={{ alignment: "left", composition: "offset" }}
    title="Next: how a bus depot rewrote its rota with the drivers"
    body="Part three of our series on operations fixes that started on the floor."
    primary="Read part three"
    secondary="All case studies"
  />

  <Footer slot="footer" companyName="Shopfloor Review" />
</BaseLayout>
